1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
From underground dungeons

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
to modern high-tech security cells

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,000
from workhouses

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,000
to work details

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,000
from heavy leg irons

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
to electronic bracelets

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
These are the ways society over the centuries

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,000
has dealt with crime through punishment.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Prisons on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:46,814 --> 00:00:50,500
Today, 8 million people are behind bars around the world,

11
00:00:50,501 --> 00:00:52,620
2 million in the United States alone.

12
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:56,340
Some are housed in high-tech maximum security fortresses.

13
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,960
Others are in more medieval-looking structures.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,120
Inmates are also secured inside prison walls through a variety of sophisticated technology.

15
00:01:05,121 --> 00:01:08,320
I think technology is the key to the future.

16
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:15,600
You've got to give officers in jail and prison systems anywhere in this country,

17
00:01:15,601 --> 00:01:21,040
the key behind their success is giving the officers the tools necessary to do their job.

18
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,840
A lot of that is about technology.

19
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,200
Microwave sensors use a transmitter that emits high frequency radio waves

20
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:34,063
between itself and a receiver. The waves form an invisible electric field.

21
00:01:34,064 --> 00:01:35,180
Anything entering

22
00:01:35,181 --> 00:01:39,960
that field disturbs the waves, which weakens the signal to the receiver and triggers an

23
00:01:39,961 --> 00:01:40,661
alarm.

24
00:01:40,662 --> 00:01:48,640
Robots equipped with explosives can blow up walls in prison riots or hostage situations.

25
00:01:48,641 --> 00:01:55,880
A lot of technology has been developed just for the correctional industry, but a lot of

26
00:01:55,881 --> 00:02:01,920
the development that has come out of defense activities has been now applied to the correctional industry.

27
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:10,060
The toughest and most incorrigible inmates are housed in super maximum security prison units,

28
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:13,040
which generally have the most advanced technology.

29
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:21,900
Super max facilities have evolved as a concept in the last 10 years to deal with some of the most problematic inmates in the system

30
00:02:21,901 --> 00:02:25,060
to get them out of the rest of the facilities in the system.

31
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:35,804
So we're just going to get them in an environment where we can really batten down the hatches, keep close control, and not worry about them infecting the rest of the

32
00:02:35,805 --> 00:02:36,520
population.

33
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:41,320
California has the majority of high-security inmates.

34
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,960
In 1990, it unveiled a state-of-the-art maximum security housing unit at Pelican Bay State Prison.

35
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,540
It became a model for the rest of the country.

36
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:56,000
Its X-shaped security housing unit was a high-tech replica of the nation's earliest prisons,

37
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:58,560
featuring solitary confinement cells.

38
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:04,300
The inmate sits in a windowless cell with a poured concrete sleeping slab

39
00:03:04,301 --> 00:03:08,860
and an immobile concrete or steel toilet behind a thick steel-plated door.

40
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:13,400
Corrections officers monitor him from control booths with video cameras

41
00:03:13,401 --> 00:03:15,280
and communicate through speakers.

42
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,200
Supermax, you typically feed an inmate in their cell.

43
00:03:19,201 --> 00:03:31,405
That means designing a food pass in the cell door or off to the side so the officer will come by, the food will be pre-portioned, put in a tray, inserted in the cell,

44
00:03:31,406 --> 00:03:34,420
collected after a certain period of time.

45
00:03:34,421 --> 00:03:43,240
Today's high-tech supermax may be a far cry from security measures used at the earliest prisons.

46
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:50,380
But throughout history, society's views on how to treat criminals can be summed up by the barriers built to house them.

47
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:56,200
It's a worthy history to study, and it tells us who we are as a people.

48
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:02,260
You can use a metaphor of the wall and the anti-wall.

49
00:04:02,261 --> 00:04:08,700
the people who believe in more confinement, more retribution,

50
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,680
in more punishment as being the people of the war.

51
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:18,260
And you can think of the people who would like to eliminate walls,

52
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,720
would like to open up the spaces,

53
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:25,620
would like to have opportunities for rehabilitations as the anti-war.

54
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:31,900
For prisons, certainly beginning in the 17th century,

55
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:35,080
you did have a modest wall around them.

56
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:39,920
Later, the walls increased in height and security.

57
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:47,020
In the 1950s and 60s, in the United States at least,

58
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:49,920
the wall became too expensive to build.

59
00:04:49,921 --> 00:04:55,460
They began to use cyclone fences and razor wire coils

60
00:04:55,461 --> 00:04:58,120
and later on, electronic devices.

61
00:04:58,121 --> 00:05:05,120
What the public expects from prison today is really the sense that the people inside are going to stay inside.

62
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,100
They go back to their images from the James Cagney movies,

63
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:12,840
and this real sense that once that door closed behind somebody, it was with a sense of finality.

64
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:21,480
Before the 18th century, prisons were nothing more than holding cells where criminals waited to be tried.

65
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:28,260
They were sentenced to death, mutilated, branded, or banished to another country.

66
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:41,700
For the most part, before the 18th century, the method of punishing felons was corporal punishment, various forms of corporal punishment, capital punishment.

67
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:48,060
Prisoners were held in crude dungeons for short periods of time until their punishment.

68
00:05:48,061 --> 00:05:51,900
Some were detained with a garrote, or iron collar,

69
00:05:51,901 --> 00:05:55,200
that would be locked around their neck to disable or torture them.

70
00:05:55,201 --> 00:05:58,200
If tightened, they would strangle to death.

71
00:05:58,201 --> 00:06:02,900
Before the 18th century, prison design was almost an afterthought.

72
00:06:02,901 --> 00:06:06,120
Various structures that were built for other purposes

73
00:06:06,121 --> 00:06:09,720
were adapted for use as holding cells,

74
00:06:09,721 --> 00:06:13,460
but had not been designed initially with the intention of making them prisons.

75
00:06:13,461 --> 00:06:17,860
The first known system of prisons was a series of dungeons

76
00:06:17,861 --> 00:06:20,100
built above the main sewers in Rome

77
00:06:20,101 --> 00:06:22,520
between the 3rd and 1st centuries BC.

78
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,580
Here, prisoners were confined in large rooms.

79
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:29,100
From the 4th century and until the Renaissance,

80
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:32,020
the Roman Catholic Church contributed greatly

81
00:06:32,021 --> 00:06:33,300
to the evolution of the prison.

82
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:35,480
Throughout Europe and in England,

83
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:38,840
church authorities imprisoned both clergy and lay people

84
00:06:38,841 --> 00:06:40,380
who violated canon law.

85
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,340
They were housed in monasteries and abbeys.

86
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:51,460
If monks committed very serious offenses, such as arson or murder, there were underground

87
00:06:51,461 --> 00:06:59,340
rooms where the monk would be confined permanently and religious tracts and food would be lowered

88
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:02,100
to him.

89
00:07:02,101 --> 00:07:07,860
I think the most important thing that is drawn from the precedence of the medieval world,

90
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:12,740
and particularly later on Elizabethan England and 18th century England and London, is the

91
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:16,900
whole idea of the notion of the cell. And I think it draws on the monastic tradition

92
00:07:16,901 --> 00:07:23,140
particularly, that this is a place where one is isolated, compelled to turn into the self

93
00:07:23,141 --> 00:07:26,460
and reflect on the self.

94
00:07:26,461 --> 00:07:31,660
This was the beginning of prison design, which centuries later would be referred to as solitary

95
00:07:31,661 --> 00:07:32,438
confinement.

96
00:07:32,439 --> 00:07:38,080
In theory, it was meant to rehabilitate an inmate through isolation and prayer, instead

97
00:07:38,081 --> 00:07:42,180
of physical punishment. Prisoners were confined to their locked cells.

98
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:53,220
The locks were the kinds of primitive locks that had been used in Roman times down through

99
00:07:53,221 --> 00:07:59,780
the Middle Ages, or they would simply place an iron bar on brackets over the door, usually

100
00:07:59,781 --> 00:08:02,780
a lock with a huge key about this large.

101
00:08:02,781 --> 00:08:07,740
Secular prisons began to emerge as early as the 12th century,

102
00:08:07,741 --> 00:08:12,100
with the rise of civil governments in Europe and the development of public law.

103
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:20,700
Castles and fortresses always were built with strong rooms on lower levels

104
00:08:20,701 --> 00:08:25,160
to store material in case the castle was under siege.

105
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:31,380
Those rooms could be used later, particularly when castles had no military value.

106
00:08:31,381 --> 00:08:33,580
they could be used to house prisoners.

107
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,780
Ruling monarchs used castles such as the Tower of London

108
00:08:41,781 --> 00:08:45,860
or France's Bastille to lock away political rivals.

109
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,740
By the 15th century, the breakup of feudalism

110
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:55,780
and the expansion of cities caused much economic and social turmoil.

111
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:57,780
Work was scarce.

112
00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:01,220
Many became homeless, turning to begging, stealing.

113
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:07,300
prostitution, acts the English government deemed as criminal. To deal

114
00:09:07,301 --> 00:09:13,000
with these new lawbreakers, the English built workhouses. The houses of

115
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:19,640
correction were called bridewells because the king had donated a sort of

116
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:25,500
rundown palace called the bridewell palace and that became a workhouse.

117
00:09:25,501 --> 00:09:33,980
Street people, vagabonds, prostitutes, and petty thieves were put in there, not in cells, but in rooms.

118
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:40,400
In those rooms, prisoners manufactured various products under strict discipline.

119
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:48,320
Prisoners worked hard, and here the idea of reform came in.

120
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,980
But not all European countries adopted the workhouse concept.

121
00:09:52,981 --> 00:09:56,640
some turned to distant outposts of civilization overseas,

122
00:09:57,440 --> 00:09:59,820
such as Australia, North Africa,

123
00:10:00,340 --> 00:10:03,360
and free American colonies like Delaware and Maryland.

124
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:08,260
Such penal colonies existed until the mid-1900s.

125
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:12,820
It was a lot cheaper for European governments

126
00:10:12,821 --> 00:10:15,400
in the late 18th and early 19th centuries

127
00:10:15,401 --> 00:10:18,400
to send miscreants to the colonies.

128
00:10:18,401 --> 00:10:26,800
Now in some cases, they were actual penal colonies that people were sent to, such as Devil's

129
00:10:26,801 --> 00:10:27,800
Island.

130
00:10:27,801 --> 00:10:35,220
Devil's Island in South America's French Guiana was legendary for its horrendous conditions

131
00:10:35,221 --> 00:10:37,780
and brutal physical punishment.

132
00:10:37,781 --> 00:10:44,500
It was a humid jungle known for its hard labor, insect-infested cells, and starving prisoners.

133
00:10:44,501 --> 00:10:49,420
But even banishment to other countries, workhouses and traditional prisons could not keep up with

134
00:10:49,421 --> 00:10:53,540
England's growing inmate population.

135
00:10:53,541 --> 00:10:58,740
Its solution was to incarcerate prisoners on derelict warships and merchant vessels,

136
00:10:58,741 --> 00:11:00,480
called hulks.

137
00:11:00,481 --> 00:11:03,620
The ships were anchored in waterways throughout Britain.

138
00:11:03,621 --> 00:11:08,260
As many as 5,000 convicts were imprisoned at any given time.

139
00:11:08,261 --> 00:11:13,840
Conditions aboard the ships were filthy, crowded and disease-ridden.

140
00:11:13,841 --> 00:11:22,020
And by all accounts, those inmates were in even more miserable conditions than were those

141
00:11:22,021 --> 00:11:25,500
in local jails.

142
00:11:25,501 --> 00:11:27,300
Some hulks were no longer seaworthy.

143
00:11:27,301 --> 00:11:33,080
They took on so much water that inmates living in their lower decks drowned.

144
00:11:33,081 --> 00:11:35,560
Prisoners wore leg irons and handcuffs.

145
00:11:35,561 --> 00:11:42,600
It is thought to be one of the first times a ball and chain was also put to use.

146
00:11:42,601 --> 00:11:47,640
The ships ultimately were not workable.

147
00:11:47,641 --> 00:11:50,540
Though Hulks vanished after the late 1800s,

148
00:11:50,541 --> 00:11:54,520
the concept resurfaced recently in a more civilized way.

149
00:11:54,521 --> 00:11:57,780
Rikers Island in New York City housed its overflow inmate

150
00:11:57,781 --> 00:12:01,000
population on ferries in the 1990s.

151
00:12:01,001 --> 00:12:05,720
We still have today two of those boats on the island

152
00:12:05,721 --> 00:12:07,520
or attached to the island.

153
00:12:07,521 --> 00:12:10,640
They hold 162 inmates each.

154
00:12:10,641 --> 00:12:14,900
And although they're at a commission right now, within a week's time,

155
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:19,240
they could be reactivated as swing space for the agency if we needed them.

156
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:25,080
The United States leads all countries with the most people behind bars,

157
00:12:25,081 --> 00:12:28,220
nearly one quarter of the world's prison population.

158
00:12:28,221 --> 00:12:40,640
Back in the mid-1800s, a wave of architectural reform swept through prisons.

159
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:47,760
Englishman Jeremy Bentham proposed new designs to make prisons more secure and easily managed.

160
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:52,980
One of the popular designs Bentham instituted was called the Panopticon Plan.

161
00:12:52,981 --> 00:13:05,400
And the arrangement of these prisons had to be that the governor could look from the windows of his central hub, house,

162
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:11,580
into all the cells that radiated like spokes of a wheel from that center.

163
00:13:11,581 --> 00:13:19,160
And the idea was that he could keep an eye not only on the inmates but also on the guards.

164
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:22,560
It didn't work very well.

165
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:30,760
The panopticon design didn't work because the architecture made it impossible for the governor to see all areas of the institution from his office.

166
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:37,720
In America, a prominent doctor named Benjamin Rush had other ideas about what a prison should look like.

167
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:46,220
He believed prisons should remove not only the dangerous people from society, but also they should try to reform inmates into law-abiding citizens.

168
00:13:46,221 --> 00:13:53,340
With that in mind, the Quakers helped establish the first true prison in America called the Walnut Street Jail,

169
00:13:53,341 --> 00:13:58,920
a place where inmates would do penance, hence the term penitentiary was created.

170
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:09,340
It consisted of a series of large rooms, but as the Walnut Street Jail became seriously overcrowded,

171
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:14,640
they planned to build a large prison, now a state prison.

172
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:19,720
This resulted in the construction of Eastern State Penitentiary in Philadelphia,

173
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:23,920
which adopted the Quaker philosophy of punishment by solitary confinement.

174
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:27,600
It later became known as the Pennsylvania System.

175
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:31,300
Eastern was a radical departure in prison architecture.

176
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:34,880
This floor plan shows its radial cell block design.

177
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:41,300
So the design was set up in this hub-and-spoke manner

178
00:14:41,301 --> 00:14:43,400
in order to improve supervision of the inmates

179
00:14:43,401 --> 00:14:46,060
and thereby prevent escapes.

180
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:52,380
The philosophy behind Eastern was to keep the inmates separate from each other

181
00:14:52,381 --> 00:14:56,120
to prevent them from forming gangs and organizing riots or escapes.

182
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:01,260
When a prisoner first arrived, he was escorted to his cell with a hood over his head

183
00:15:01,261 --> 00:15:04,780
so that he could not see the other prisoners or where he was going,

184
00:15:04,781 --> 00:15:07,520
and they, in turn, could not see him.

185
00:15:07,521 --> 00:15:14,300
The prison was designed in the way it was, was to ensure that the prison could carry

186
00:15:14,301 --> 00:15:20,680
out its philosophy of solitary confinement for all the inmates.

187
00:15:20,681 --> 00:15:27,080
By 1820 standards, Eastern was massive, with nearly 11 acres inside its walls.

188
00:15:27,081 --> 00:15:33,560
The outside wall was one half mile around, and eight feet thick at the base.

189
00:15:33,561 --> 00:15:36,200
Eastern was one of the first prisons to build towers.

190
00:15:36,201 --> 00:15:40,940
The front building was 200 feet long, with a square tower turret at each end.

191
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:45,000
It also had a guard tower on the roof of its central rotunda.

192
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:50,960
The cells were quite large, large enough that an inmate would not only have his living space,

193
00:15:50,961 --> 00:15:58,240
but also room for a workbench, a loom, machinery, tools, raw materials,

194
00:15:58,340 --> 00:16:03,120
whatever he needed in order to do his assigned work,

195
00:16:03,121 --> 00:16:06,920
which could be everything from weaving or dyeing cloth,

196
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:10,140
making shoes, caning chairs, things of this nature.

197
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:13,300
It all was done in the individual cell.

198
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:18,060
But the hub and spoke architecture of Eastern Penitentiary

199
00:16:18,061 --> 00:16:19,680
had many design flaws.

200
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:25,540
It was expensive to design, build, and operate a prison of this kind

201
00:16:25,541 --> 00:16:28,520
because the rooms, the individual cells, had to be so large.

202
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:34,160
and also because having the inmates by themselves, working by themselves,

203
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:38,340
they could not take advantage of the techniques of factory production.

204
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:45,020
So there was less opportunity to make money for the inmates to produce revenue for the institution.

205
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:51,220
Not everyone adopted the Pennsylvania system of solitary confinement that operated at Eastern.

206
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:55,540
A different approach towards prisons was taken in neighboring New York.

207
00:16:55,541 --> 00:16:59,540
Authorities there abandoned the solitary confinement approach

208
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:03,540
and built a prison in Auburn, New York, in 1825,

209
00:17:03,541 --> 00:17:05,540
that housed convicts together.

210
00:17:05,541 --> 00:17:08,540
It was called the Auburn or Congregate Silence System.

211
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:12,540
The most well-known and one of the first prisons

212
00:17:12,541 --> 00:17:15,540
to create this system was Sing Sing,

213
00:17:15,541 --> 00:17:18,540
north of New York City on the Hudson River.

214
00:17:18,541 --> 00:17:20,540
The prison became known for the terms

215
00:17:20,541 --> 00:17:22,540
Up the River and the Big House.

216
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:32,900
If you're going to have the men work in congregate, that is in a group setting, and the reason

217
00:17:32,901 --> 00:17:37,740
that was chosen here is because it was productive and cost effective.

218
00:17:37,741 --> 00:17:43,800
Contracts could be let, inmates could be organized into shops, those shops would be monitored

219
00:17:43,801 --> 00:17:47,220
by supervisors appointed by the contractor.

220
00:17:47,221 --> 00:17:50,780
The state was in favor of that because it generated revenues.

221
00:17:50,781 --> 00:17:53,660
Those revenues offset the cost of the institution.

222
00:17:53,661 --> 00:17:59,880
The state thought also it was ferryman with the skills necessary to serve in the outside world.

223
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:03,340
One of those skills was construction work.

224
00:18:04,040 --> 00:18:08,440
In 1825, New York State had the inmates who would be sentenced to live in Sing Sing

225
00:18:08,441 --> 00:18:11,800
build the prison from limestone in a nearby quarry.

226
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:14,480
It took them three years.

227
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:18,560
This is a work that had a very strange reward.

228
00:18:18,561 --> 00:18:23,980
as the cell walls got higher and the cell blocks got more finished,

229
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:26,920
there must have been a sense of completing one's own imprisonment.

230
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:29,820
I think there was some sense of justice in that.

231
00:18:32,980 --> 00:18:36,460
Sing Sing's cell block was 476 feet long,

232
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,400
four tiers high, and had more than 800 cells.

233
00:18:41,300 --> 00:18:44,880
Cell blocks consisted of long, multi-tiered double rows of cells

234
00:18:44,881 --> 00:18:46,520
that were placed back to back.

235
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:52,340
The cells were so small, many inmates had to walk in sideways.

236
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:57,200
Because the inmates were out of the cells during the day,

237
00:18:57,640 --> 00:19:00,740
and they were working in a congregate type of environment,

238
00:19:00,741 --> 00:19:03,240
either in one of the shops or in the quarries.

239
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:05,960
And at night, they would go back to their cells,

240
00:19:06,040 --> 00:19:07,560
and the idea with cells was you could sleep,

241
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:11,320
and you didn't need to do anything pretty much else besides do that.

242
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:15,720
The cells were three feet wide and seven feet deep.

243
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:18,760
They had no running water and only a bucket for a toilet.

244
00:19:19,460 --> 00:19:23,200
For more than 100 years, it was Sing Sing's main cell block.

245
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:28,960
In 1926, when bank robber Willie Sutton served time there,

246
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:30,240
he wrote,

247
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:32,720
Very little sunlight penetrated the block,

248
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:36,000
and the only ventilation was through the pattern

249
00:19:36,001 --> 00:19:38,360
of one-inch square holes in the doors.

250
00:19:38,361 --> 00:19:45,800
This is all that remains of Sing Sing's original cell block today.

251
00:19:45,801 --> 00:19:53,480
They realized that they could put more inmates in a given space if they stacked them on top

252
00:19:53,481 --> 00:19:57,963
of each other. So they built cells on top of cells.

253
00:19:57,964 --> 00:20:01,040
To control the swinging grill doors

254
00:20:01,041 --> 00:20:05,900
from a central location at the end of the tier, there's this device at the top of the cells.

255
00:20:05,901 --> 00:20:12,240
In later years, a new locking technology appeared, which was less vulnerable to tampering.

256
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:17,600
It let staff open 50 cells at one time by pulling a lever.

257
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:20,920
It was safer and more efficient for prison staff.

258
00:20:22,900 --> 00:20:26,020
The advantage was that you could open all the cells at the same time.

259
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:26,780
It was a new thing.

260
00:20:26,781 --> 00:20:33,500
You didn't have to individually key lock or key open each cell, which was what you had to do before that.

261
00:20:33,501 --> 00:20:40,500
But if an officer had to open a cell individually, he would use a 5-inch long paracentric key.

262
00:20:40,501 --> 00:20:43,500
It has grooves which cover over the center of the key.

263
00:20:43,501 --> 00:20:48,500
It originated in the early 1900s to open the levered tumbler lock.

264
00:20:48,501 --> 00:20:51,500
Some prisons, like Sing Sing, still use it today.

265
00:20:51,501 --> 00:20:58,500
The paracentric key was later upgraded to today's oversized style, called a Maxi Mogul key.

266
00:20:58,501 --> 00:21:01,900
It is used for pin tumbler locks at more modern prisons

267
00:21:01,901 --> 00:21:04,340
and is harder for inmates to replicate.

268
00:21:07,140 --> 00:21:08,960
Sing Sing was one of the first prisons

269
00:21:08,961 --> 00:21:11,040
to require that inmates wear the traditional

270
00:21:11,041 --> 00:21:12,640
black and white striped uniform.

271
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:15,460
It was considered a security measure.

272
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:19,280
If somebody escaped, you knew they were an inmate,

273
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:20,840
unless they could find some other clothes to wear.

274
00:21:22,360 --> 00:21:25,180
Inmates at Sing Sing were also secured with leg irons

275
00:21:25,181 --> 00:21:28,460
that were 5 feet long and weighed 25 pounds each.

276
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:33,260
But despite the restraints, they still managed to find ways to escape from Sing Sing.

277
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:37,340
The most popular was by hopping the fence and catching the train,

278
00:21:37,460 --> 00:21:40,200
which ran and still runs through the prison grounds.

279
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:43,420
They were always captured eventually.

280
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:50,800
If recapture was so certain, why, in fact, did they persist two and three a week?

281
00:21:51,480 --> 00:21:54,680
And the reason was because I think it was an act of resistance,

282
00:21:54,681 --> 00:22:00,700
in which when the whistle blew here in the 19th century

283
00:22:00,701 --> 00:22:03,500
announcing an inmate had attempted to escape,

284
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,220
there was enormous cheer from the population.

285
00:22:06,800 --> 00:22:08,640
All kinds of obscenities would be heard,

286
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:10,800
and the roar lasted for several minutes.

287
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:14,200
And everybody knew that tomorrow they would see him again,

288
00:22:14,201 --> 00:22:17,040
but what they really knew was that somebody had stood up to the system.

289
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:21,680
Sing Sing was a model for prisons around the world.

290
00:22:22,200 --> 00:22:24,200
It was legendary for its electric chair,

291
00:22:24,680 --> 00:22:27,100
The freeze the last mile originated here.

292
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:30,020
600 inmates were electrocuted at Sing Sing,

293
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:34,500
including the most famous, A-bomb spies Julius and Ethel Rosenberg.

294
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:37,160
I'm doing life.

295
00:22:37,660 --> 00:22:40,400
199 years, so no pardon, no parole.

296
00:22:40,820 --> 00:22:42,880
And you can't crash over those walls in a million years.

297
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:45,440
The only chance is to get to the courthouse and try it from there.

298
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:47,560
Where do I come in?

299
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:52,680
Sing Sing's intimidating facade made it a favorite location

300
00:22:52,681 --> 00:22:55,020
for Hollywood directors in their prison movies.

301
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:58,040
Many of the 19th century prisons were built

302
00:22:58,041 --> 00:23:02,040
with a massive, bastille-like cell block design

303
00:23:02,041 --> 00:23:03,920
in order to be intimidating,

304
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:07,020
to demonstrate the power of the state,

305
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:09,480
to scare people away,

306
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:11,340
and to show the inmates who went there

307
00:23:11,341 --> 00:23:15,480
that they were going to a very intimidating,

308
00:23:15,900 --> 00:23:17,200
unpleasant sort of a place.

309
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:20,600
The number of inmates returning to prison

310
00:23:20,601 --> 00:23:22,000
after committing new offenses

311
00:23:22,001 --> 00:23:27,440
made it clear to authorities that the harshness of incarceration did not deter crime.

312
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:31,840
At the time it was constructed in the 1820s,

313
00:23:32,340 --> 00:23:36,220
Eastern State Penitentiary was the most expensive building in the United States.

314
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:39,520
Cost? $770,000.

315
00:23:39,521 --> 00:23:40,751
dollars.

316
00:23:40,752 --> 00:23:52,600
As unsatisfactory as prisons may have been for men in the early 20th century,

317
00:23:52,601 --> 00:23:54,816
they were far worse for women.

318
00:23:54,817 --> 00:23:59,100
Typically, female prisoners spent their time in basements,

319
00:23:59,560 --> 00:24:02,211
attics, and corners of male prisons.

320
00:24:02,212 --> 00:24:06,260
An English Quaker, Elizabeth Gertie Frye, believed that

321
00:24:06,261 --> 00:24:11,060
female offenders could be rehabilitated, but only if they were isolated from the corrupting

322
00:24:11,061 --> 00:24:17,880
influence of men by keeping them in prisons for females only, run by a female staff.

323
00:24:17,881 --> 00:24:21,524
Women prisoners had work assignments in home-like settings.

324
00:24:21,525 --> 00:24:23,500
Cottages or dormitories replaced

325
00:24:23,501 --> 00:24:27,260
the traditional cell blocks.

326
00:24:27,261 --> 00:24:31,260
And the notion of these as institutions without walls probably has a lot to do with the so-called

327
00:24:31,261 --> 00:24:36,100
cottage system. They look like small towns. They would have a commons.

328
00:24:36,101 --> 00:24:40,790
a large open public space. The cottages would be clustered around each other.

329
00:24:40,791 --> 00:24:41,491
There would

330
00:24:41,492 --> 00:24:47,560
also be usually a common dining hall. The success of the cottage system led to

331
00:24:47,561 --> 00:24:51,660
other women's institutions, which provided religious and vocational guidance.

332
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:59,600
Like the women, black inmates in the South also worked in the early 20th century, but

333
00:24:59,601 --> 00:25:05,600
not necessarily to be rehabilitated. Many states leased inmates to work on farms and

334
00:25:05,601 --> 00:25:07,640
help build highways, mostly in the South.

335
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:12,320
The convicts got the name chain gangs because their ankles were chained together as they

336
00:25:12,321 --> 00:25:13,021
worked.

337
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:21,420
In the 1920s and 30s, the nation experienced a crime wave related to the failure of prohibition

338
00:25:21,421 --> 00:25:23,080
and the rise of gangsters.

339
00:25:23,240 --> 00:25:29,420
The public demanded hoodlums like Al Capone and Machine Gun Kelly be locked up in an escape-proof

340
00:25:29,421 --> 00:25:30,121
prison.

341
00:25:30,122 --> 00:25:41,260
In 1934, the Federal Bureau of Prisons sent these criminals to a newly remodeled former military prison on an island in San Francisco Bay called Alcatraz.

342
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:49,100
They took that prison and made it a very secure prison.

343
00:25:49,900 --> 00:25:55,420
The prison, of course, was very expensive to run. It was an old physical plant.

344
00:25:55,421 --> 00:26:02,420
Over the years, Alcatraz had its share of prison myths and high-profile residents.

345
00:26:02,421 --> 00:26:07,420
One of them was convicted killer Robert Stroud, the notorious birdman of Alcatraz.

346
00:26:07,421 --> 00:26:14,420
Prison staff say he had a Jekyll and Hyde personality and was far from being the gentle scientist portrayed in the movies.

347
00:26:14,421 --> 00:26:16,753
They say he didn't even like birds.

348
00:26:16,754 --> 00:26:20,420
He only raised them as a way of hiding various weapons.

349
00:26:20,421 --> 00:26:25,420
Alcatraz was the answer at the time to an important correctional solution.

350
00:26:25,421 --> 00:26:30,520
Authorities recognized that it was a small number of inmates, less than 1% of the entire

351
00:26:30,521 --> 00:26:35,440
prison population, that posed such serious management problems that whole prisons had

352
00:26:35,441 --> 00:26:38,600
to be devoted to controlling them.

353
00:26:38,601 --> 00:26:44,820
The result was to create the nation's first super maximum security prison and lock up all

354
00:26:44,821 --> 00:26:50,260
the worst criminals there without an attempt at rehabilitation.

355
00:26:50,261 --> 00:26:57,920
They retrofitted it, put in new tamper-resistant bars, took out the old-fashioned strap-iron

356
00:26:57,921 --> 00:27:04,560
bars, put in new locking devices, made it a much more secure facility than it had been before.

357
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:08,268
But still, that did not prevent escapes.

358
00:27:08,269 --> 00:27:12,340
During its 30 years of operation, Alcatraz saw more than

359
00:27:12,341 --> 00:27:15,428
a dozen escapes. None were considered successful.

360
00:27:15,429 --> 00:27:18,580
Many were attributed to the prison's deteriorating

361
00:27:18,581 --> 00:27:19,384
conditions.

362
00:27:19,385 --> 00:27:25,960
Obviously, if you're in the middle of a bay and salt water and you're a concrete facility,

363
00:27:25,961 --> 00:27:28,262
you're going to have some deterioration.

364
00:27:28,263 --> 00:27:31,600
But that was the technology that was available at the time

365
00:27:31,601 --> 00:27:32,804
that that was built.

366
00:27:32,805 --> 00:27:37,200
It was so costly to repair Alcatraz that the government closed the prison

367
00:27:37,201 --> 00:27:37,901
in 1963.

368
00:27:37,902 --> 00:27:43,780
But its closure led quickly to the creation of more and greater super maximum

369
00:27:43,781 --> 00:27:44,780
security prisons.

370
00:27:44,781 --> 00:27:52,260
So although it was picturesque and it captured everybody's imagination, it was abandoned,

371
00:27:52,261 --> 00:27:59,540
and they began to build from the ground up a high security prison in Marion, Illinois.

372
00:27:59,541 --> 00:28:05,620
The Federal Penitentiary in Marion was a structure designed in the telephone pole

373
00:28:05,621 --> 00:28:07,780
style of architecture.

374
00:28:07,781 --> 00:28:12,760
This called for its cell blocks, workshops, recreational facilities, chapels and dining

375
00:28:12,761 --> 00:28:17,720
areas to extend from each side of a long central corridor.

376
00:28:17,721 --> 00:28:20,720
Unlike Alcatraz, rehabilitation of inmates

377
00:28:20,721 --> 00:28:24,220
was reestablished as a goal of imprisonment.

378
00:28:24,221 --> 00:28:28,360
But its philosophy was short-lived.

379
00:28:28,361 --> 00:28:30,920
Marion had tremendous gang problems

380
00:28:30,921 --> 00:28:32,380
and had tremendous violence problems

381
00:28:32,381 --> 00:28:34,440
within the facility.

382
00:28:34,441 --> 00:28:36,220
The violence escalated in Marion,

383
00:28:36,221 --> 00:28:38,860
as well as many prisons across the country.

384
00:28:38,861 --> 00:28:41,200
In the late 1960s, the political culture

385
00:28:41,201 --> 00:28:47,280
of civil disobedience and non-negotiable demands led to more than 100 prison outbreaks in 1969

386
00:28:47,281 --> 00:28:48,424
and 1970 alone.

387
00:28:48,425 --> 00:28:54,600
The most famous riot occurred in 1971 at Attica State Prison in western New York.

388
00:28:55,220 --> 00:28:58,597
11 officers and 32 inmates were killed.

389
00:28:58,598 --> 00:29:03,360
Michigan State Prison in Jackson endured several riots.

390
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:08,235
A series of assaults and deaths on staff and inmates occurred at Merriam.

391
00:29:08,236 --> 00:29:09,200
The prison was put

392
00:29:09,201 --> 00:29:14,700
on permanent lockdown status in 1983.

393
00:29:14,701 --> 00:29:16,540
It became the nation's first prison

394
00:29:16,541 --> 00:29:19,300
to create a super maximum security unit,

395
00:29:19,301 --> 00:29:22,400
where incorrigible inmates were housed in a single cell

396
00:29:22,401 --> 00:29:24,840
for 23 hours a day.

397
00:29:24,841 --> 00:29:26,900
When corrections authorities around the country

398
00:29:26,901 --> 00:29:30,200
saw how successful Marion was in reducing violence,

399
00:29:30,201 --> 00:29:33,400
almost every state created high security units.

400
00:29:33,401 --> 00:29:43,220
In the early to mid-60s, there was a real focus on rehabilitation and rehabilitative environments.

401
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:50,960
More recently, we've moved more towards an approach to warehousing and building harder and harder facilities.

402
00:29:51,520 --> 00:29:56,540
In some ways, in response to what the population is we're dealing with today,

403
00:29:57,160 --> 00:30:01,060
and in other ways, simply in response to what public concerns are about what prisons are.

404
00:30:02,060 --> 00:30:05,720
The warehouse-like structures of Supermax units today

405
00:30:05,721 --> 00:30:08,500
have high-tech features and modern cell designs,

406
00:30:08,680 --> 00:30:10,900
as well as more correctional officers.

407
00:30:12,020 --> 00:30:16,120
So this typically involves smaller housing units rather than larger ones.

408
00:30:16,400 --> 00:30:20,360
Let's say you take someone because they have a visit with their attorney or family.

409
00:30:20,361 --> 00:30:24,220
There would be two officers to accompany that inmate,

410
00:30:24,820 --> 00:30:29,080
and they have a walkway above the regular corridor that looks down.

411
00:30:29,081 --> 00:30:29,922
that's a grate.

412
00:30:29,923 --> 00:30:34,300
There's an officer above with a weapon and tear gas in case there's a problem.

413
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:42,040
So they actually have a separate secure corridor system that an officer will walk in to accommodate

414
00:30:42,041 --> 00:30:42,741
that.

415
00:30:42,742 --> 00:30:49,160
Since its creation, the Supermax has become one of the fastest growing types of prisons built

416
00:30:49,161 --> 00:30:49,863
in America.

417
00:30:49,864 --> 00:30:55,100
I think what the public expects to see is something which clearly tells the public

418
00:30:55,101 --> 00:30:59,240
that they're safe, that the people that are inside aren't going to get outside.

419
00:31:00,060 --> 00:31:03,600
You know, if we go back, historically, our hardest prisons were the walled institutions

420
00:31:03,601 --> 00:31:05,900
with towers, the Bastille kind of prison.

421
00:31:06,560 --> 00:31:11,120
Today, that's really been approached by, you know, double fences, multiple coils of razor

422
00:31:11,121 --> 00:31:13,520
ribbon, perimeter detection systems.

423
00:31:13,521 --> 00:31:19,880
The supermax prisons now make no pretense at reform.

424
00:31:19,881 --> 00:31:27,720
That is, it's considered a quarantine to protect the staff and the inmates from their behavior.

425
00:31:28,720 --> 00:31:34,180
Preventing inmates at Supermax and other prisons from escaping requires special security system.

426
00:31:35,580 --> 00:31:41,180
More than 16,000 inmates live in Supermaximum security units in the United States.

427
00:31:41,181 --> 00:31:53,120
Imprisonment today, as it did 200 years ago, means separation from the free world.

428
00:31:53,121 --> 00:31:58,420
But the methods of keeping that separation have become more technological and specific in design.

429
00:31:58,740 --> 00:32:03,540
What we focus on when we design a prison is really security in three areas.

430
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:07,140
Obviously, the primary mission is the security of the public.

431
00:32:07,700 --> 00:32:10,580
But equally important is the safety of the staff.

432
00:32:10,581 --> 00:32:14,160
We like to think of the staff as somebody that's sentenced there to serve a life sentence

433
00:32:14,161 --> 00:32:17,720
eight hours a day, five days a week for as long as they're with the system.

434
00:32:18,140 --> 00:32:23,400
And finally, the safety of the inmate, which is also the responsibility of the state who's operating the facility.

435
00:32:25,180 --> 00:32:30,420
What's important in prison design is that it makes it easy for officers to see inmates in a cell block.

436
00:32:30,980 --> 00:32:36,260
Some architects provide 3D computer animation to prison authorities before construction

437
00:32:36,261 --> 00:32:40,660
so that they can confirm that the structure being built will serve their needs.

438
00:32:43,320 --> 00:32:47,780
Well, one of the important things in designing a facility, a correctional facility,

439
00:32:47,781 --> 00:32:53,500
is for the officers, they're supposed to be supervising what is going on.

440
00:32:54,300 --> 00:32:57,500
The primary way we do that is to make sure they can see everything.

441
00:32:57,880 --> 00:33:00,140
The second thing is acoustically being able to hear.

442
00:33:00,220 --> 00:33:04,700
In a housing unit, we try to design these so that an officer in a unit

443
00:33:04,701 --> 00:33:09,100
can kind of be aware of what's going on around him

444
00:33:09,101 --> 00:33:11,420
just in terms of noise level, activity level.

445
00:33:11,540 --> 00:33:13,320
If the fight breaks out or a scuffle,

446
00:33:13,600 --> 00:33:15,980
he can hear that, he can respond to it immediately.

447
00:33:17,140 --> 00:33:20,200
The most common approach to maximum visual design

448
00:33:20,201 --> 00:33:21,560
is a central control room.

449
00:33:23,100 --> 00:33:25,920
Basically, the officer sits in a glass room

450
00:33:25,921 --> 00:33:28,680
that has remote controls and remote communication

451
00:33:28,681 --> 00:33:29,700
in the housing units.

452
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:33,120
This design is called a pod.

453
00:33:33,121 --> 00:33:37,460
The officer sits at the center surrounded by cells on both sides.

454
00:33:38,040 --> 00:33:40,060
Surveillance cameras line the cell blocks.

455
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:48,320
As a result of the riots that were taking place across the country in the 70s,

456
00:33:48,600 --> 00:33:57,220
there were changes in the designing for what is the most useful number of inmates to have in any housing area

457
00:33:57,221 --> 00:34:01,560
and what is the best way to arrange the configuration.

458
00:34:02,360 --> 00:34:05,260
And around the country, you'll see the modular units.

459
00:34:07,120 --> 00:34:10,420
The design and construction of cells have also changed.

460
00:34:11,320 --> 00:34:13,600
Some jails, like Rikers Island and New York City,

461
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:17,060
use cells that are prefabricated in a factory in Pennsylvania.

462
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:19,680
They are made of concrete and steel

463
00:34:19,681 --> 00:34:23,820
and are delivered by truck in units which consist of two cells

464
00:34:23,821 --> 00:34:26,220
joined by a plumbing and electrical closet.

465
00:34:27,220 --> 00:34:31,020
The cell units are hoisted by crane into a prepared steel structure,

466
00:34:31,320 --> 00:34:32,920
which is the framework of the jail.

467
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:37,800
The precast construction also serves as a safety feature

468
00:34:37,801 --> 00:34:43,440
by making it almost impossible for an inmate to break off any part of the cell to fashion a weapon.

469
00:34:47,180 --> 00:34:51,660
Next to the cell is a safety feature that provides security for prison staff,

470
00:34:52,180 --> 00:34:54,120
should the cell have a maintenance problem.

471
00:34:54,121 --> 00:35:02,320
In the past, if we had a plumbing problem in a cell or a flood, we had to shut down a string of cells.

472
00:35:02,940 --> 00:35:08,580
This advantage allows us to work directly with that individual cell for plumbing and electrical problems

473
00:35:08,581 --> 00:35:15,140
without closing off cells or removing inmates out of adjacent cells while we do repairs.

474
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:22,200
Outside, today's penitentiaries are now surrounded by a new, more lethal generation of the old barbed wire,

475
00:35:22,201 --> 00:35:23,527
razor ribbon wire.

476
00:35:23,528 --> 00:35:28,980
Usually it is surrounded by a new version of a perimeter fence that is now

477
00:35:28,981 --> 00:35:31,947
electronically upgraded with taut wire.

478
00:35:31,948 --> 00:35:36,360
The wire is double braided with a breaking strength of 950

479
00:35:36,361 --> 00:35:41,442
pounds. It is attached to steel posts anchored into the ground.

480
00:35:41,443 --> 00:35:43,620
It is connected to a device

481
00:35:43,621 --> 00:35:48,915
called a tensioner which keeps the wires tight to 65 pounds of force.

482
00:35:48,916 --> 00:35:50,680
If the wires are cut or

483
00:35:50,681 --> 00:35:54,020
tampered with, the sensors set off an alarm.

484
00:35:54,021 --> 00:35:56,380
If an inmate attempts to escape, alarms

485
00:35:56,381 --> 00:36:00,180
will pick up a signal that goes to a central control room,

486
00:36:00,181 --> 00:36:01,920
which dispatches an officer.

487
00:36:06,040 --> 00:36:08,200
But with the advent of technology,

488
00:36:08,201 --> 00:36:12,980
we were able to install electronic sensors

489
00:36:12,981 --> 00:36:17,500
around the perimeter fence so that now we can take the officers

490
00:36:17,501 --> 00:36:21,620
out of the guard towers use their talents in other ways.

491
00:36:22,620 --> 00:36:25,500
Facilities such as the Arizona State Prison in Lewis

492
00:36:25,501 --> 00:36:30,020
also use buried sensor cables which transmit electromagnetic energy.

493
00:36:30,800 --> 00:36:35,600
If an intruder is in the area, an alarm goes off instantly at the central control station.

494
00:36:37,100 --> 00:36:43,340
Sensors such as the microwave intrusion link can detect intruders from as far away as 1,500 feet.

495
00:36:43,341 --> 00:36:48,040
Despite all the high-tech devices at prisons today,

496
00:36:48,580 --> 00:36:53,260
inmates with time on their hands do manage to outsmart officers and technology.

497
00:36:54,700 --> 00:36:56,200
You know, when I worked in Illinois,

498
00:36:56,320 --> 00:37:00,040
we had three very dangerous gang members walk right out the front door.

499
00:37:00,740 --> 00:37:02,640
They worked in the clothing room,

500
00:37:03,160 --> 00:37:08,360
and our process was to give an individual a suit of clothes upon release.

501
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:11,740
And so they made clothes, and they made an extra set.

502
00:37:11,741 --> 00:37:15,260
and during a tour they just joined the tour and walked right out.

503
00:37:16,100 --> 00:37:21,060
A unit of two prefabricated cells weighs nearly 26 tons.

504
00:37:22,120 --> 00:37:26,040
Each cell costs about $9,500 to build and install.

505
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:37,920
Breaking into prison is almost as hard as breaking out.

506
00:37:37,921 --> 00:37:46,920
In many institutions, biometric devices, technology which uses parts of the body, are now in use.

507
00:37:46,921 --> 00:37:50,920
The hand geometry device electronically reads a handprint on a computer.

508
00:37:50,921 --> 00:37:55,920
The prison gate will only open for those whose handprints are in its system.

509
00:37:55,921 --> 00:38:01,920
Eyes can also be used to identify prison staff or inmates.

510
00:38:01,921 --> 00:38:08,140
The iris scan biometric device is a device that actually takes a picture of the iris.

511
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:17,360
It then looks at the patterns of the iris and correlates those patterns to a previously recorded pattern of that particular individual.

512
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:26,020
If the correlation is a positive or a match, then it authorizes the event to take place, which is typically maybe opening a door.

513
00:38:28,020 --> 00:38:31,200
Eyes are also part of the technology inside prisons.

514
00:38:31,920 --> 00:38:39,300
Correctional officers now have a way to distract inmates during a riot with a device called the laser dazzler.

515
00:38:39,960 --> 00:38:45,220
It's a bright laser light that can temporarily blind an inmate and make him nauseous.

516
00:38:49,500 --> 00:38:59,620
And in order to eliminate a cell search, a sophisticated detector can trace fingerprints or small particles from narcotics in a cell just by wiping a surface.

517
00:39:01,920 --> 00:39:08,480
But when officers are shut out of a cell block during a riot or hostage situation, they can

518
00:39:08,481 --> 00:39:13,900
now tape what's going on inside as it's going on with the help of television technology

519
00:39:13,901 --> 00:39:16,320
called a video multiplex.

520
00:39:16,321 --> 00:39:20,180
It can show 16 cameras at once throughout the prison.

521
00:39:20,181 --> 00:39:24,520
The camera system can be linked to a phone line, so if one screen shows problems at a

522
00:39:24,521 --> 00:39:29,260
particular location in the prison, authorities can call into that camera, hear what's going

523
00:39:29,261 --> 00:39:35,200
on and communicate.

524
00:39:35,201 --> 00:39:38,140
Officers can also see what's going on in a darkened cell

525
00:39:38,141 --> 00:39:41,600
block during a riot with a device called Tiger Vision.

526
00:39:41,601 --> 00:39:44,440
It's a night imaging camera that allows them to record what

527
00:39:44,441 --> 00:39:48,820
they see without using a light.

528
00:39:48,821 --> 00:39:50,780
And to keep inmates under control,

529
00:39:50,781 --> 00:39:54,540
the latest in restraints is this Velcro ankle bracelet,

530
00:39:54,541 --> 00:39:56,380
which comes with a matching shoulder harness.

531
00:39:59,260 --> 00:40:06,100
Today, prisons can control individual cells at a central panel.

532
00:40:06,101 --> 00:40:10,260
Not only can correctional officers open and close cell doors by the touch of a computer

533
00:40:10,261 --> 00:40:15,320
screen, they can also control the flow of water in an inmate's toilet.

534
00:40:15,321 --> 00:40:22,180
So we developed technology that allowed us to control how many times a toilet would flush,

535
00:40:22,181 --> 00:40:24,060
when it would become inactive.

536
00:40:24,061 --> 00:40:29,260
When we wanted to flush it from a remote location, if we felt an inmate was storing urine in

537
00:40:29,261 --> 00:40:33,640
feces in his toilet so that he could throw it on a staff member, we could remotely flush

538
00:40:33,641 --> 00:40:34,677
that toilet.

539
00:40:34,678 --> 00:40:40,900
If an inmate has been involved in flooding the pod, we could control how

540
00:40:40,901 --> 00:40:45,275
many times that toilet could be flushed from a central location.

541
00:40:45,276 --> 00:40:47,120
So every water device could

542
00:40:47,121 --> 00:40:48,491
be changed individually.

543
00:40:48,492 --> 00:40:51,920
And we found that was helpful technology in just running the

544
00:40:51,921 --> 00:40:56,620
institution safer and more efficiently.

545
00:40:56,621 --> 00:41:01,980
At Rikers Island, correctional officers use an electronic immobilization shield, which

546
00:41:01,981 --> 00:41:09,600
sends 50,000 volts of electricity to temporarily stun an uncontrollable inmate.

547
00:41:09,601 --> 00:41:15,200
Prior to 1995, if there was a problem with an inmate in a cell, the method of removing

548
00:41:15,201 --> 00:41:21,700
that inmate was to send a five-man probe team in and physically fight the inmate to get

549
00:41:21,701 --> 00:41:29,080
removed from the cell. Now, instead of strip searching an inmate for weapons, the

550
00:41:29,081 --> 00:41:35,700
Body Orifice Security System, or BOSS Chair, can detect any metal objects an inmate might

551
00:41:35,701 --> 00:41:38,082
hide anywhere on the body.

552
00:41:38,083 --> 00:41:43,580
Innovative things like that have been a very successful tool

553
00:41:43,581 --> 00:41:48,560
to sending a clear message to the inmates that you can't possess weapons. If they're

554
00:41:48,561 --> 00:41:54,140
They're caught with a weapon looking through the boss chair system.

555
00:41:54,540 --> 00:41:59,780
They're charged with the crime of bringing contraband and possessing contraband in the system.

556
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:06,120
When there's a violation of the prison security system, authorities react quickly to correct it.

557
00:42:07,440 --> 00:42:13,280
Prison officials are also quick to change technology at their institutions when a breach of security occurs.

558
00:42:13,281 --> 00:42:20,280
In 1997, a correctional officer at the Arizona State Prison in Perryville was stabbed to

559
00:42:20,281 --> 00:42:23,280
death by an inmate who was able to unlock his cell door.

560
00:42:23,281 --> 00:42:30,280
So they could actually spin the lock by just putting something in the lock, spinning it,

561
00:42:30,281 --> 00:42:31,928
and it released the door.

562
00:42:31,929 --> 00:42:36,280
After we had the incident, we went back and changed those devices.

563
00:42:36,281 --> 00:42:42,280
And not only at that facility, but any other facility in the state that had the same design

564
00:42:42,281 --> 00:42:43,280
design flaw.

565
00:42:46,280 --> 00:42:51,280
The Arizona Department of Corrections changed the cell locks, as well as those on control

566
00:42:51,281 --> 00:42:56,420
room doors, so they would automatically lock behind a correctional officer without a key.

567
00:43:00,380 --> 00:43:05,920
Because of extreme overcrowding and the huge costs of operating prisons today, a method

568
00:43:05,921 --> 00:43:09,120
of confining minor offenders outside prison is underway.

569
00:43:09,121 --> 00:43:12,120
It's basically a system of house arrest.

570
00:43:12,121 --> 00:43:16,120
An offender lives at home with an electronic bracelet strapped to his ankle.

571
00:43:16,121 --> 00:43:23,120
The bracelet sends out radio signals to a small receiver located in his home and workplace.

572
00:43:23,121 --> 00:43:27,120
The receiver transmits a signal to a computer in a monitoring center,

573
00:43:27,121 --> 00:43:31,120
which has the offender's exact schedule so authorities can keep track of him.

574
00:43:31,121 --> 00:43:36,120
It is the ultimate high-tech concept of prison without walls.

575
00:43:36,121 --> 00:43:44,140
The history of correction in the United States can be told in the struggle that goes on,

576
00:43:44,180 --> 00:43:48,560
and still goes on, between the forces of the war and the forces of anti-war.

577
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:55,980
And general common reasoning in our society is that one solution does not fit all.

578
00:43:57,220 --> 00:44:00,660
So the war has its purpose and its value.

579
00:44:00,661 --> 00:44:05,780
and the people who say there are better ways to go,

580
00:44:06,380 --> 00:44:07,900
they have their victories.

581
00:44:08,420 --> 00:44:13,900
And this struggle of good, intentioned and honest people

582
00:44:13,901 --> 00:44:18,020
is what makes the whole subject so fascinating.

583
00:44:19,140 --> 00:44:20,880
In the years to come,

584
00:44:21,460 --> 00:44:24,820
society will look to a new generation of correctional officers,

585
00:44:25,240 --> 00:44:26,880
architecture and technology

586
00:44:26,881 --> 00:44:30,100
to create more positive chapters in prison history.

587
00:44:30,660 --> 00:44:39,120
Looking back on the past, hopefully we'll help prison authorities capture new insights and lessons for controlling crime and punishment.

